- Focus groups in local languages;
- Interviews with professional and employee representatives;
- Workflow observation;
- Approved-tool telemetry;
- Security and network indicators of unapproved tool use;
- Help-desk and access data;
- Manager behavior assessments;
- Tool performance by language and jurisdiction;
- Analysis of data-classification and approval barriers.

Results should be segmented by country, function, seniority, role, age only where lawful and useful, language, manager, and workflow. The purpose is to identify barriers—not build cultural rankings.

### Useful adoption archetypes

| Pattern | Likely causes | Primary response |
|---|---|---|
| Open enthusiasts | Strong local sponsorship, usable tools, low perceived career threat | Channel enthusiasm into validated workflows and peer coaching |
| Compliant but superficial users | Usage quotas, fear of appearing resistant, poor workflow fit | Remove activity pressure and redesign relevant work |
| Covert users of approved tools | Fear of judgment, inconsistent telemetry, local norms against appearing dependent on AI | Normalize responsible use and explain monitoring transparently |
| Shadow-AI users | Approved tools are slower, weaker, inaccessible, or unsuitable for local languages | Safe disclosure, migrate use cases, improve the toolkit, enforce data controls |
| Professionally skeptical users | Pride in expertise, concern about quality or deskilling | Position AI as an assistant; use expert-led validation and preserve sign-off |
| Privacy or ethics resisters | Unclear data handling, customer confidentiality, intrusive telemetry | Publish technical and legal controls; provide restricted environments |
| Headcount-fear resisters | Recent cost programs, ambiguous leadership messages | Workforce compact, role pathways, reskilling, honest workforce planning |
| Hierarchical passive resisters | Employees will not challenge a senior mandate openly | Confidential channels, local leader modeling, no-retaliation protection |
| Collectively resistant teams | Strong professional or labor identity and low trust in headquarters | Early consultation, local pilots, negotiated safeguards |
| Low-use but high-performing specialists | AI adds little to current work or use is episodic | Permit role-based targets and assess outcomes rather than frequency |
| High-use but low-quality teams | Overreliance, weak review, metric gaming | Tighten review controls, coaching, quality gates, and risk monitoring |

These archetypes should guide inquiry, not become fixed labels attached to countries or individuals.

