## 4. Correct the performance-dashboard design

The dashboard is the highest trust risk in the proposed mandate. It should be redesigned before becoming part of formal performance management.

### Three-layer measurement model

#### Layer 1: Enablement and compliance

Measure whether employees have:

- Completed required training;
- Received appropriate access;
- Acknowledged acceptable-use rules;
- Completed role-based assessment;
- Used approved rather than unauthorized tools;
- Reported incidents appropriately.

These are legitimate mandatory indicators.

#### Layer 2: Workflow adoption

Measure use at the workflow or team level, such as:

- Percentage of eligible processes using an approved AI capability;
- Number of validated use cases in production;
- Active adoption among eligible users;
- Reuse of approved prompts, agents, or templates;
- Percentage of AI-assisted work that follows required review steps.

Adoption denominators must reflect role eligibility. A claims lawyer, actuary, receptionist, field adjuster, and software developer should not have the same expected usage profile.

#### Layer 3: Outcomes and safeguards

Measure:

- Cycle-time reduction;
- Rework and correction rates;
- Accuracy and quality;
- Customer satisfaction and complaints;
- Claims leakage or decision consistency;
- Underwriting turnaround time;
- Employee workload and engagement;
- Accessibility gains;
- Security or privacy incidents;
- Unsupported citations or hallucinations;
- Bias and disparate customer outcomes;
- Productivity gains actually realized;
- Time returned to higher-value activities.

No productivity benefit should be claimed unless workload, quality, or capacity has measurably changed.

### Rules for individual performance use

For at least the first six months:

- Use individual telemetry for learning, support, security, and compliance—not comparative performance ranking.
- Do not publish individual leaderboards.
- Do not set prompt-volume targets.
- Do not infer effort, attitude, or competence from inactivity.
- Do not allow automated employment recommendations based on usage.
- Require human review and documented context before any employment consequence.
- Provide employees access to the data attributed to them and a correction process.
- Publish who can see each metric, how long it is retained, and for what purposes it may be used.

After the learning period, formal performance discussions may consider whether an employee is using required approved workflows safely. Raw volume should still carry little or no weight. Any proposed use of AI-generated monitoring or profiling for performance evaluation should undergo privacy, employment, discrimination, information-security, and applicable AI-regulation review.

### Prevent metric gaming

The group should monitor for signs that the mandate itself is distorting behavior:

- Repetitive low-value prompts;
- Unnecessary AI use;
- Generated content discarded immediately;
- Inflated “time saved” estimates;
- Employees doing work outside the system and entering it later;
- Reduced escalation of tool errors;
- Shared accounts;
- High use without quality improvement.

A dashboard that rewards activity will create activity. A dashboard that rewards verified outcomes and safe behavior is more likely to create value.

