## 6. Tailor change interventions to local cultural patterns

Global standards should remain consistent, but the way the change is introduced should vary.

### High-hierarchy environments

Risks include silent compliance, hidden resistance, and reluctance to report AI errors.

Use:

- Visible executive admission that AI outputs can be wrong;
- Private question channels;
- Anonymous challenge exercises;
- Manager training against coercive usage targets;
- Independent ethics and privacy contacts;
- Local champions who are respected but not part of the direct reporting chain.

Leaders should ask, “What should stop us from using this here?” rather than, “Why have you not adopted it?”

### Consensus-oriented or employee-representation environments

Risks include delay if employees perceive consultation as symbolic.

Use:

- Early works-council, union, professional-body, or employee-forum engagement;
- Joint privacy and monitoring reviews;
- Agreed pilot protocols;
- Published impact assessments;
- Representative employee participation in tool selection;
- Negotiated rules for telemetry and role redesign.

Consultation should occur before decisions are operationally irreversible.

### Individualistic, competitive environments

Risks include metric gaming, hoarding useful prompts, and overuse to signal productivity.

Use:

- Team-based outcome measures;
- Recognition for reusable assets and peer coaching;
- Quality and risk counterweights;
- No individual activity leaderboards;
- Rewards for identifying where AI should not be used.

### Relationship- and trust-oriented environments

Risks include interpreting a headquarters mandate as loss of local autonomy or surveillance.

Use:

- Local senior sponsors and face-to-face demonstrations;
- Country-specific examples;
- Trusted peer advocates;
- Clear assurance that local management remains involved;
- Communication in local language and culturally credible formats.

### Strong professional-identity functions

Actuaries, underwriters, claims specialists, medical reviewers, lawyers, auditors, and compliance professionals may see AI as an attack on expertise.

Use:

- Expert-led rather than technology-led pilots;
- Position AI as reducing search, formatting, comparison, and administrative work;
- Preserve named professional sign-off;
- Test outputs against expert benchmarks;
- Recognize detection of AI errors as valuable work;
- Teach “reviewing AI” as an advanced professional skill;
- Monitor deskilling and maintain independent analytical capability.

Cultural adaptation should alter communication, participation, and support—not lower privacy, security, customer-protection, or professional standards.

