## 11. Implementation roadmap

### Phase 0: Reset the mandate—first 30 days

- Pause use of raw activity metrics in individual performance ratings;
- Publish the definition of mandatory use;
- Confirm approved and prohibited tools;
- Launch privacy, legal, regulatory, and employee-relations assessments;
- Begin consultation where required;
- Create the global and country governance bodies;
- Communicate that low usage will initially trigger inquiry and support, not automatic penalty;
- Announce the safe-disclosure process for shadow AI.

### Phase 1: Diagnose and build trust—months 1–3

- Complete country and function diagnostics;
- Publish the workforce compact and telemetry notice;
- Identify cultural and workflow archetypes;
- Test approved tools across languages and accessibility needs;
- Select low-risk, high-value workflows;
- Train managers and local champions;
- Correct access and integration problems;
- Establish baseline quality, cycle-time, engagement, and risk metrics.

### Phase 2: Pilot by workflow—months 3–6

- Pilot within representative countries and functions;
- Use expert-led output validation;
- Measure time saved and work actually removed;
- Compare telemetry with employee feedback;
- Convert useful shadow-AI practices into approved solutions;
- Conduct privacy, security, fairness, and professional reviews;
- Publish both successes and failures.

### Phase 3: Scale with local adaptation—months 6–12

- Make validated workflows mandatory for eligible roles;
- Retain documented exceptions;
- Roll out country-specific communications and training;
- Introduce team-level adoption dashboards;
- Monitor quality, workload, customer outcomes, and unauthorized use;
- Expand role-transition and advanced-skill programs;
- Require quarterly country leadership reviews.

### Phase 4: Integrate into performance carefully—after month 12

Only after independent review should performance processes consider:

- Completion of required training;
- Compliance with approved workflows;
- Quality of human review;
- Responsible escalation;
- Contribution to workflow improvement and knowledge sharing;
- Achievement of relevant business and customer outcomes.

Raw prompt volume, login time, or generated content should remain excluded or carry negligible weight.

### Phase 5: Continuous assurance

- Revalidate tools and use cases as models change;
- Audit access, prompts, retention, and data flows;
- Monitor deskilling and automation bias;
- Reassess workforce impacts;
- Refresh cultural diagnostics;
- Review whether local exceptions remain necessary;
- Retire low-value use cases;
- Report material incidents and corrective actions to the appropriate boards.

