## 12. Success measures

### Adoption and capability

- Training and assessment completion;
- Percentage of eligible workflows using approved tools;
- Active use by eligible role, not total workforce;
- Reuse of approved templates and agents;
- Number of shadow workflows migrated to approved environments;
- Employee confidence in reviewing AI output.

### Trust and culture

- Employee understanding of telemetry;
- Confidence that concerns can be raised safely;
- Perceived fairness of the mandate;
- Fear of job loss and surveillance;
- Manager coercion or retaliation reports;
- Differences in trust and adoption across countries and functions;
- Number and resolution time of telemetry challenges.

### Business outcomes

- Verified cycle-time reduction;
- Capacity released and how it was redeployed;
- Rework rates;
- Quality and accuracy;
- Customer satisfaction and complaint outcomes;
- Claims and underwriting turnaround;
- Reduction in administrative burden;
- Financial benefits net of licensing, integration, review, and remediation costs.

### Risk and customer protection

- Unauthorized-tool incidents;
- Sensitive-data exposure;
- Hallucination and unsupported-citation rates;
- Material professional-review failures;
- Bias or disparate-outcome findings;
- Incorrect customer communications;
- Security incidents;
- Regulatory findings;
- Percentage of high-risk use cases with current approval and testing.

### Workforce outcomes

- Reskilling and redeployment rates;
- Workload and burnout;
- Critical-skill retention;
- Voluntary attrition;
- Internal mobility;
- Role redesign completed;
- Employee participation in use-case design.

All metrics should be reviewed for misleading comparisons. For example, low usage may reflect low workflow eligibility, stronger data restrictions, poor language support, or a highly efficient specialist team—not resistance.

